<h1>Medical Device Operating Manual</h1>

<PhHighlightedContent Type="HighlightedContentType.Info">
    <strong>Important:</strong> Read all safety instructions and operational procedures before using this medical device. Proper training and certification are required for all operators.
</PhHighlightedContent>

<h2>Device Setup and Preparation</h2>

<p>Before operating the device, ensure that all connections are secure and the workspace is properly prepared. The device should be placed on a stable, level surface away from sources of electromagnetic interference.</p>

<img class="image-left" src="https://picsum.photos/400/300?random=100" alt="Medical device setup" width="400" height="300" loading="lazy" />

<p>Connect the power cable to a grounded electrical outlet and verify that the power indicator light is green. Check that all accessory components are properly attached and that the sample chamber is clean and ready for use. The device will automatically run self-diagnostics upon startup.</p>

<p>Calibration should be performed daily or according to your laboratory's quality control procedures. Ensure that certified reference materials are available and within their expiration dates before beginning any patient testing procedures.</p>

<PhHighlightedContent Type="HighlightedContentType.Warning">
    <strong>Caution:</strong> Never attempt to operate the device with damaged cables or loose connections. Electrical hazards may result in equipment damage or personal injury. Contact technical support if any components appear damaged.
</PhHighlightedContent>

<h3>Sample Processing Guidelines</h3>

<p>Proper sample handling is critical for accurate results. All samples must be properly labeled with patient identification, collection time, and test requirements. Follow your laboratory's chain of custody procedures at all times.</p>

<p>Sample types supported by this device include:</p>

<ul>
    <li>Serum samples (preferred volume: 500&#181;L minimum)</li>
    <li>Plasma samples (EDTA or heparin anticoagulant)</li>
    <li>Whole blood samples (for specific test panels only)</li>
    <li>Urine samples (first morning void recommended)</li>
</ul>

<p>Ensure samples are at room temperature before processing. Cold samples may require 15-30 minutes to reach optimal temperature. Hemolyzed, lipemic, or icteric samples may affect test results and should be noted in the patient record.</p>

<PhHighlightedContent Type="HighlightedContentType.Error">
    <strong>Critical Alert:</strong> Biological samples may contain infectious agents. Always wear appropriate personal protective equipment including gloves, lab coats, and safety glasses. Follow universal precautions and your facility's biohazard disposal procedures for all sample materials.
</PhHighlightedContent>

